<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oath of Pontida</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1167">1167</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oath of Pontida is the traditional founding act of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombard-league">Lombard League</a>, said to have been sworn on 7 April 1167 at the abbey of Pontida near Bergamo by representatives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, Cremona, Bergamo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, Mantua and other cities. They pledged mutual aid against the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, whose army the league defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnano">Legnano</a> in 1176. The oath is known only from later sources.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oath-of-pontida.jpg" alt="Oath of Pontida" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ago76 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pontida_monastero_benedettino_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Chronicles written between 1152 and 1189, during the struggle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, record that the communes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombard-league">Lombard League</a> made pacts of mutual aid against the emperor's dominance, but mostly without naming places or dates, and none of them mentions an oath at Pontida. The Piacenza chronicle notes that in 1167 all the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and the March except Pavia agreed to rebuild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, which the emperor had destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The most precise contemporary sources describe at least three oaths in 1167. The first, known as the Bergamo oath, was sworn between the end of February and the beginning of March by Bergamo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, Cremona and Mantua; in the second, in March, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> joined them; and in the third, in May, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> joined the coalition. Since two of these came before 7 April, an oath at Pontida, even if it was sworn, cannot have been the act that founded the league.</p><p>It remains possible that other agreements were made whose record was lost, since an alliance was rarely settled in a few sessions. Pontida also had a close tie to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>: although it lay in the county of Bergamo, it belonged to the archdiocese of Milan, so an oath sworn there cannot be ruled out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The oath first appears in the Historia Patria of Bernardino Corio, a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> dated 1505, some 350 years after the traditional date, and later accounts drew on it. A codex of 1584 credits the archbishop of Milan, Umberto Pirovano, with holding a meeting of the neighbouring cities in the church of San Giacomo at Pontida to further the rebuilding of Milan, and Giuseppe Antonio Sassi repeated the story in 1755. Each June about a hundred people in costume re-enact the events in Pontida's Giuramento square, and since 1990 the Lega Nord party has held a yearly meeting there celebrating the oath.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oath of Pontida” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1167, according to tradition in an oath sworn at <b>Pontida</b>, Milan, Cremona, Bergamo, Brescia, Mantua and other cities formed a league against Frederick Barbarossa, absorbing the Veronese League.</blockquote><a href="/state/lombard-league">Lombard League</a></li></ul></section>
